            <ArticleTitle>Musculoskeletal etiologies of pelvic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>433-42</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ogc.2014.04.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0889-8545(14)00035-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Several musculoskeletal diagnoses are frequently concomitant with pelvic floor pathology and pain. The definition of pelvic pain itself often depends on the medical specialist evaluating the patient. Because there is variability among disorders associated with pelvic pain, patients may seek treatment for extended periods as various treatment options are attempted. Further, health care providers should recognize that there may not be a single source of dysfunction. This article discusses the musculoskeletal disorders of the pelvic girdle (structures within the bony pelvis) and their association with lumbar spine and hip disorders.</AbstractText>
